```java
import java.util.*;

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();   // contiguous, cache-friendly
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) list.add(i);

long sum = 0;
for (int v : list) {        // O(1) per element via the iterator
    sum += v;               // O(n) total, no node walking
}
```
If you truly need a list and must traverse a LinkedList, use a for-each/iterator (O(n) total), never `get(i)` in a loop.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class GetComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 50_000;
        List<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> lnk = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { arr.add(i); lnk.add(i); }

        // ArrayList: O(1) per get -> O(n) total
        long s1 = 0; for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) s1 += arr.get(i);

        // LinkedList: O(n) per get -> O(n^2) total (slow!)
        long s2 = 0; for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) s2 += lnk.get(i);

        // Correct way to traverse a LinkedList: for-each is O(n)
        long s3 = 0; for (int v : lnk) s3 += v;
    }
}
```

